{% extends "pages/_guide.jinja" %}
{% set guide_slug = 'binding-and-filtering' %}
{% set thesis = 'A scenario becomes a test when something binds it: #[scenario] for one, scenarios! for a directory. Tags decide which, and skip! says why one did not run.' %}
{% set sections = [
  {'id': 'scenario', 'label': 'One scenario'},
  {'id': 'directory', 'label': 'A whole directory'},
  {'id': 'tags', 'label': 'Tag expressions'},
  {'id': 'skipping', 'label': 'Skipping'},
  {'id': 'fine-print', 'label': 'Fine print'},
] %}
{% block guide %}
          <section id="scenario" aria-labelledby="scenario-title">
            <h2 id="scenario-title">One scenario</h2>
            <p>
              <code>#[scenario]</code> turns a function into the test for one
              scenario. <code>path</code> names the feature file, relative to
              the crate root; <code>name</code> picks a scenario by title, or
              <code>index</code> by position, and without either the first
              scenario is used.
            </p>
            {% call ui.code_panel('tests/hamper.rs', sample='tests/hamper.rs') %}
            {% highlight 'rust', 'lantern-syntax' %}
#[scenario(path = "tests/features/hamper.feature", name = "Pack the picnic")]
fn pack_the_picnic(hamper: Hamper) {
    let _ = hamper;
}
            {% endhighlight %}
            {% endcall %}
            <p>
              The function’s parameters are the scenario’s fixtures, and its
              body runs after the last step. A name that matches no scenario,
              or more than one, is a compile error.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section id="directory" aria-labelledby="directory-title">
            <h2 id="directory-title">A whole directory</h2>
            <p>
              <code>scenarios!</code> walks a directory and generates a test for
              every scenario it finds, named from the file and the scenario
              title. Fixtures the steps need are listed with their types, since
              there is no function signature to declare them.
            </p>
            {{ ui.sample('tests/weather.rs') }}
            {% call ui.output('cargo test --test weather', 'rstest-bdd ' ~ rstest_bdd_version) %}
running 2 tests
test auto_scenarios::weather_thunder_in_the_hills ... ok
test auto_scenarios::weather_a_dry_evening ... ok

test result: ok. 2 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.00s
            {% endcall %}
          </section>

          <section id="tags" aria-labelledby="tags-title">
            <h2 id="tags-title">Tag expressions</h2>
            <p>
              <code>tags = "…"</code> filters by the tags on the feature, the
              scenario, and an Outline’s Examples table. Expressions use
              <code>and</code>, <code>or</code>, <code>not</code>, and
              parentheses, with <code>not</code> binding tightest and
              <code>or</code> loosest.
            </p>
            {{ ui.sample('tests/features/auto/weather.feature') }}
            <p>
              <code>"@smoke and not @wip"</code> keeps the dry evening and the
              thunder, and leaves the fog out; there are two tests above, not
              three. The filter runs when the test compiles, so a left-out
              scenario is absent from the test list rather than ignored.
              <code>#[scenario]</code> takes the same <code>tags</code>
              argument, and fails to compile when the scenario it names does
              not satisfy it.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section id="skipping" aria-labelledby="skipping-title">
            <h2 id="skipping-title">Skipping</h2>
            <p>
              A step that cannot sensibly go on calls
              <code>rstest_bdd::skip!</code> with a reason. The remaining steps
              and the scenario body do not run. The thunder scenario above does
              exactly that:
            </p>
            {% call ui.code_panel('tests/weather.rs', sample='tests/weather.rs') %}
            {% highlight 'rust', 'lantern-syntax' %}
#[given("the forecast is \"{forecast}\"")]
fn forecast(evening: &mut Evening, forecast: String) {
    if forecast == "thunder" {
        bdd::skip!("no picnics in a thunderstorm");
    }
    evening.forecast = forecast;
}
            {% endhighlight %}
            {% endcall %}
            <p>
              A skipped scenario still reports <code>ok</code>, which is easy to
              miss. Set <code>RSTEST_BDD_FAIL_ON_SKIPPED=1</code> and every skip
              fails the test, unless its feature or scenario carries
              <code>@allow_skipped</code>, as the thunder does.
            </p>
            {% call ui.code_panel('Terminal', copy='console') %}
            {% highlight 'console', 'lantern-syntax' %}
$ RSTEST_BDD_FAIL_ON_SKIPPED=1 cargo test --test weather
            {% endhighlight %}
            {% endcall %}
            <p>
              <code>cargo bdd skipped --reasons</code> lists every skip and its
              reason; see <a href="/rstest-bdd/guides/tooling/#cargo-bdd">tooling</a>.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section id="fine-print" aria-labelledby="fine-print-title">
            <h2 id="fine-print-title">Fine print</h2>
            {% call ui.note('Where skip! may be called', 'caveat') %}
            <p>
              <code>skip!</code> works inside a step or a helper a step calls,
              on the thread running the step. Called anywhere else, it panics
              and says so.
            </p>
            {% endcall %}
          </section>
{% endblock %}
